## Freeze-boundary clarification

v0.5.0 freezes the **complete semantics**, while the full v0.5.x line performs the freeze/versioning/compatibility hardening.

The v0.5.0 freeze must start from a v0.4 implementation where these are already real product behavior, not future intentions:

- runtime/workload convergence is trustworthy;
- supported artifact/image drift is observable and reconcilable;
- repository source changes cannot leave a stale successful workload;
- provider-neutral application intent is established (logical capability first; product/provider realization separate);
- developer-visible provider/runtime realization remains transparent;
- guided init + `baha up` is the canonical human happy path;
- CLI/verbose/JSON/MCP derive from one semantic truth.

The freeze does **not** mean hiding provider/product information. It means preventing provider products such as PostgreSQL from becoming the portable application contract while still exposing the selected provider, version, compatibility and realized artifact identity where useful.

#175 freezes the boundaries/inventory. #297–#300 then version, migrate, formalize compatibility, upgrade/recovery/deprecation and prove the frozen model.

## Contract-freeze dependency: #390

Before v0.5.x freezes the public/core contract, #390 must settle the standards-first service/binding/provider boundaries.

The freeze must not lock in accidental product/protocol identities such as:
- cache == Redis;
- object-storage == S3;
- observability == OTLP;
- identity == a concrete IdP.

Existing v0.4 IDs remain compatible until migration is explicitly tested. v0.5 freezes the resolved service-contract and compatibility model, not an unreviewed rename.
